-- title:
--   Class number of a curve as P(1)
-- statement:
--   Let $k$ be a finite field, $K$ an algebraically closed field, and $F_0$, $F$ fields with algebra maps $k \to F_0$, $K \to F$ and $F_0 \to F$, such that both $F_0/k$ and $F/K$ satisfy [`AlgebraicCurve.IsCurveOver`](def/AlgebraicCurve_IsCurveOver.html#L15): every nonzero element has a divisor of degree $0$ recording its orders at all places, every place has residue field finite over the base field, and the module of Kähler differentials is free of rank one. Assume $F_0$ is generated over $k$, as an intermediate field, by a finite set; that $\mathrm{ConstantsAreBase}$ holds for $k \subseteq F_0$, i.e. the Riemann–Roch space of the zero divisor is exactly the image of $k$ in $F_0$; and that the $K$-subfield of $F$ generated by the image of $F_0$ is all of $F$. Let $\varphi : F \to F$ be a $K$-algebra endomorphism which is integral as a ring homomorphism and satisfies $\varphi(\mathrm{alg}_{F_0 \to F}(x)) = \mathrm{alg}_{F_0 \to F}(x^{\#k})$ for all $x \in F_0$; write $\mathrm{Fr}$ for the induced self-map `Place.restrictAlong` of the places of $F/K$, pulling back a valuation subring along $\varphi$. Let $P \in \mathbb{Z}[X]$ be monic and suppose that for every $n \ge 1$ the number of fixed points of $\mathrm{Fr}^{n}$ equals, in $\mathbb{C}$, $(\#k)^n + 1 - \sum_{\omega} \omega^{n}$, the sum running over the complex roots of $P$ with multiplicity. Then $P(1) = \#\mathrm{Pic}^0(F_0/k)$ in $\mathbb{Z}$, where $\mathrm{Pic}^0$ is the group of degree-zero divisors modulo principal divisors.
--
--   This is F. K. Schmidt's evaluation of the $L$-polynomial of a function field of one variable over a finite field at $t = 1$ as the class number, packaged so that the input is any monic integer polynomial whose root power sums govern the counts of places of $F/K$ fixed by the iterated relative Frobenius. It is used in the derivation of the statement expressing the class number of $\mathrm{Pic}^0$ as a resultant attached to the Frobenius pushforward, and rests on the construction of the $L$-polynomial from the zeta function and on the count of fixed places by degrees.
-- source:
--   https://github.com/anthropics/fermats-last-theorem/blob/aa2d8b34692b16c70f699536de0d8e75b9a3e9ef/Theorems/Thm_AlgebraicCurve_eval_one_eq_natCard_pic0_of_natCard_fixedPoints_restrictAlong_eq.lean

